WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.522
Mitigation should be risk-based, tested, and operationally realistic.

00:00:03.522 --> 00:00:06.943
Post-quantum cryptography is one part of the response,

00:00:06.943 --> 00:00:12.075
especially for public-key cryptographic roles that may be vulnerable in the future.

00:00:12.075 --> 00:00:18.063
But PQC adoption should be standards-based and implemented through mature libraries and supported protocols.

00:00:18.063 --> 00:00:21.485
Teams should avoid custom cryptography, unsupported algorithm choices,

00:00:21.485 --> 00:00:23.195
and private protocol designs,

00:00:23.195 --> 00:00:28.755
because unreviewed mechanisms can create new risk while trying to solve future risk.

00:00:28.755 --> 00:00:31.322
Crypto agility is another key mitigation.

00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:34.743
A crypto-agile system can change algorithms, keys, libraries,

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:37.737
or protocols without redesigning the whole architecture.

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:41.158
That requires configuration discipline, versioned dependencies, clear ownership,

00:00:41.158 --> 00:00:43.725
test environments, rollback plans, and monitoring.

00:00:43.725 --> 00:00:47.146
Hybrid cryptography may also appear during transition periods,

00:00:47.146 --> 00:00:49.712
conceptually combining classical and post-quantum mechanisms.

00:00:49.712 --> 00:00:53.134
Hybrid approaches still require standards-aware implementation, compatibility testing,

00:00:53.134 --> 00:00:53.989
and governance.

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:56.555
Data lifecycle management can reduce exposure.

00:00:56.555 --> 00:00:59.549
If sensitive data is not retained unnecessarily,

00:00:59.549 --> 00:01:04.254
it cannot be harvested from that location for long-term future exposure.

00:01:04.254 --> 00:01:08.103
Retention review, minimization, segmentation, access control, secure deletion processes,

00:01:08.103 --> 00:01:10.242
and archive governance all contribute.

00:01:10.242 --> 00:01:13.235
Key rotation may help in some contexts,

00:01:13.235 --> 00:01:17.940
but teams should understand what rotation does and does not protect.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:24.783
Changing keys does not automatically erase risk from traffic or data already captured under older assumptions.

00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.777
Practical migration uses pilots and staged deployment.

00:01:27.777 --> 00:01:32.054
Teams can start with lower-risk systems, test interoperability, measure performance,

00:01:32.054 --> 00:01:35.475
review logging, coordinate with vendors, and document results.

00:01:35.475 --> 00:01:41.035
Vendor readiness tracking is important because many cryptographic dependencies live in cloud services,

00:01:41.035 --> 00:01:44.457
products, libraries, appliances, and platforms outside direct control.

00:01:44.457 --> 00:01:48.734
The best mitigation program combines cryptographic migration with data minimization,

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:51.300
operational testing, and clear decision records.
